A: Possibly not answering your real question, but rather correcting a mis-apprehension.
You state that "since the function is only called in a parallel loop, and not parellised itself, the compiler calls an error: error: ‘#pragma’ is not allowed here." However, that is simply wrong. Provided you compile with OpenMP enabled, you can use an OpenMP atomic operation anywhere, it does not have to be visible to the compiler that the code is inside any parallelism at all. Consider code like this:- void lockedIncr(int *p)
{
  #pragma omp atomic
  (*p)++;
} Since we're compiling that completely on its own, the compiler clearly cannot know the context of where it will be invoked. If you look here on Compiler Explorer, you can see it being successfully compiled for X86_64, and generating the code one would expect:- lockedInc(int*):
        lock add        DWORD PTR [rdi], 1
        ret
