std::vector<Cell> toLive; to std::vector<std::vector<Cell>> toDie;
std::vector<std::vector<Cell>> toLive; Then, in your critical section, you can just do: toDie.push_back(std::move(myToDie));
toLive.push_back(std::move(myToLive)); Arguably, a vector of vector isn't cute, but this will prevent deep-copying inside the CS which is unnecessary time consumption. [Update]
IMHO there's no point in using multithreading if you are using non-contiguous data structures, at least not in that way. The fact is you'll spend most of your time waiting on cache misses because that's what associative containers do, and little doing the actual work.
