A: This is more of an extended comment than an answer. Find the iteration limit (ie the variable among box1 , box2 , etc ) with the largest value.  Revise your loop nest so that the outermost loop runs over that.  Simply parallelise the outermost loop.  Choosing the largest value means that you'll get, in the limit, an equal number of inner loop iterations to run for each thread. Collapsing loops, whether you can use OpenMP's collapse clause or have to do it by hand, is only useful when you have reason to believe that parallelising over only the outermost loop will result in significant load imbalance.  That seems very unlikely in this case, so distributing the work (approximately) evenly across the available threads at the outermost level would probably provide reasonably good load balancing.
