A: The documentation for _mm256_store_ps says: Store 256-bits (composed of 8 packed single-precision (32-bit) floating-point elements) from a into memory. mem_addr must be aligned on a 32-byte boundary or a general-protection exception may be generated . You can use _mm256_storeu_si256 instead for unaligned stores. A better option is to align all your arrays on a 32-byte boundary (for 256-bit avx registers) and use aligned load and stores for maximum performance because unaligned loads/stores crossing a cache line boundary incur performance penalty. Use std::aligned_alloc (or C11 aligned_alloc , memalign , posix_memalign , whatever you have available) instead of malloc(size) , e.g.: float* allocate_aligned(size_t n) {
    constexpr size_t alignment = alignof(__m256);
    return static_cast<float*>(aligned_alloc(alignment, sizeof(float) * n));
}
