A: OpenMP doesn't work like this. Idiomatically you use OpenMP to make the loop in increment1 run faster, e.g. by applying #pragma omp parallel for . Of course the specific worksharing approach depends on what your actual function does. If you do need to use functional parallelism, i.e. run different functions at the same time, all of the functions that are supposed to run simultaneously (therefore including the code doing the printf ) need to be in section s (or task s).
A: You can use omp tasks: int main() {
#pragma omp parallel
  {
#pragma omp single
    {
#pragma omp task
      increment1(300000000);
#pragma omp task // if you leave out this pragma the printf will be executed 
                 // by the same thread that created the increment1 task
                 // otherwise it can be scheduled by any OMP thread
      printf("continue\n");

#pragma omp taskwait
    }
  }
  return 0;
} This example will create two tasks (one for the increment1 function and one for printf("continue\n") which will be distributed among the OMP threads. The tasks might be scheduled in any order, but when there are more OMP threads than tasks they will be scheduled concurrently. Additional note: The #pragma omp single directive is there so that the tasks are only created by one thread. other wise each thread would create a task for increment1 and the printf statement.

A: There are at least two ways I can think of doing this. One is to parallelize over the permute function and the other is over the rank . This answer uses the second method.  For n = strlen(str) the number of permutations (aka ranks) is n! .  E.g. for str = "ABCD" the number of ranks is 24 .  Here is one method to do this over rank (based on this paper ): #include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define SWAP(a,b) do{t=(a);(a)=(b);(b)=t;}while(0)

void get_permutation(int rank, int n, char *vec) {
    int t, q, r;
    if (n < 1) return;
    q = rank / n;
    r = rank % n;
    SWAP(vec[r], vec[n-1]);
    get_permutation(q, n-1, vec);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
  char a[5] = "ABCD", t[5];

  #pragma omp parallel for private(t) schedule(dynamic)
  for (int r = 0; r < 24; ++r) {
    strcpy(t, a);
    get_permutation(r, 4, t);
    #pragma omp critical
    printf("%3d: %s\n", r, t);
  }
} As long as get_permutation is slower than the output (in this case printf ) this method should win in performance.  I this will be true for a sufficiently large string length. The output on my system is 3: BCAD
