A: reset() is intended to replace the shared object reference not modify the object itself. Internally shared_ptr uses an atomic for reference counting which will increment on assignments, copies, etc and decrement as destructors are called and things go out of scope until finally destroying the managed object and releasing memory. Reset or swap is used to replace the underlying object managed with another instance. *foo = obj; should make a deep copy provided the assignment operator is overloaded to do so for the managed object in question. In your example a copy of the int value. foo = maked_shared<int>(fooPriv); may be what you want for the initial copy perhaps? In that case you would want to ensure firstOfThread is false after the initialization and then merely share the reference across the other threads so they can update the value. Preferably within a critical section to avoid tearing and race conditions. This is just general guidance as without knowing what errors you encountered or having a specific question it's hard to pinpoint exactly what you're looking for. Edit: Update based on error log. It looks like it tried to free an invalid pointer. This can be caused by a double delete or lost reference. With a shared pointer you could initialize it before the parallel section and rely on the reference counting as it's passed to each thread stack to keep it in scope. Then protect any reads and writes within the thread function to avoid race conditions. #include <iostream>
#include <boost/shared_ptr.hpp>
using namespace std;

typedef boost::shared_ptr<int> intPtr;

int main(int argc, char **argv) {

  intPtr foo = intPtr(new int(-1));

#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(2) firstprivate(foo)
  for(int i = 0; i<10; i++){
#pragma omp critical
    *foo = i;
    cout << *foo  << " ";
  }
  // join or wait for threads to complete here, if needed with OpenMP
} // foo gets deleted P.S. We have shared_ptr in standard c++11 and newer if your compiler and tool chain supports it as well. It's based off the boost implementation and works the same way.

