A: You can not reliably call any Python C-API code (which underlies pybind11), without holding the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL). Handing the GIL in your OpenMP loop for each access on each thread will effectively serialize the loop, but now with added overhead, so it will be slower than running it serially in the first place. As for interned strings: the Python interpreter saves common immutable objects such as certain strings and small integers to prevent them from being created over and over again. Such common strings are said to be "interned", and this typically happens under the hood (although you can add your own using PyString_InternFromString / PyUnicode_InternFromString ). Since these are singleton objects by design (that's their purpose, after all), only one thread should create/delete them.
