A: The main issue you have is that your code has loop-carried dependencies, i.e. there is a dependency between iterations. So your code is: for(long i = last; i >= Idx; i--)
{
  pArray[i] = pArray[i-1];
} Now, let's say last = 4 and Idx=1 . You would have something like: iteration 0: pArray[4] = pArray[3];
  iteration 1: pArray[3] = pArray[2];
  iteration 2: pArray[2] = pArray[1];
  iteration 3: pArray[1] = pArray[0]; If you parallelize this with four threads (let's say statically) and thread 0 is assigned iteration 0, thread 1 with iteration 1, and so on, you would have incorrect results depending on which thread executes first. If thread 0 executes before thread 1, thread 0 would use the old value for pArray[3] , whereas if thread 0 executes after thread 1, thread 0 would use the new value for pArray[3] , calculated by thread 1. Since your iterations are not independent, the loop cannot be parallelized straightforward. Since apparently all you want is to move the values of your array one position forward, I think a better approach would be to use pointer arithmetic or reorganize your loop and other pieces of your code to try to either eliminate the dependency or the loop entirely.
A: What you want is to do memmove in parallel.  Here is an example that does what you want. #include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <omp.h>

#define N 20

int main() {  
    int x[N+1];
    for(int i=0; i<N; i++) x[i] = i+1;
    for(int i=0; i<N; i++) printf("%d ", x[i]); puts("");
    #pragma omp parallel
    {
        int ithread = omp_get_thread_num(), nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();
        size_t pos = ithread*N/nthreads;
        size_t n = (ithread+1)*N/nthreads - pos - 1;
        int tmp = x[pos+n];
        memmove(&x[pos+1], &x[pos], sizeof *x*n);
        #pragma omp barrier
        x[pos+n+1] = tmp;   
    }
    for(int i=0; i<N+1; i++) printf("%d ", x[i]); puts("");
}
