A: Not everything that can be parallelized, should be parallelized.  If you are using a single, then only one thread executes it and the rest have to wait until the region is done.  They can either spin-wait or sleep.  Most implementations start out with a spin-wait, hoping that the single region will not take too long and the waiting threads can see the completion faster than if sleeping.  Spin-waits eat up a lot of processor cycles.  You can try specifying that the wait should be passive - but this is only in OpenMP V3.0 and is only a hint to the implementation (so it might not have any effect).  Basically, unless you have a lot of work in the parallel region that can compensate for the single, the single is going to increase the parallel overhead substantially and may well make it too expensive to parallelize.
