A: When writing concurrently to a shared variable ( a in your code), you have a data race. To avoid different threads writing "simultaneously", you must either use an atomic assignment or protect the assignment with a mutex (= mutual exclusion). In OpenMP, the latter is done via a critical region int a = 0;
#pragma omp parallel
{
#pragma omp critical
  {
    a++;
  }
}
cout << a << endl; (of course, this particular program does nothing in parallel, hence will be slower than a serial one doing the same). For more info, read the openMP documentation! However, I would advise you to not use OpenMP, but TBB if you're using C++. It's much more flexible.
A: What you are seeing is the typical example of a race condition . Four threads are trying to increment variable a and they are fighting for it. Some 'lose' and they are not able to increment so you see a result lower than 4. What happens is that the a++ command is actually a set of three instructions: read a from memory and put it in a register, increment the value in the register, then put the value back in memory. If thread 1 reads the value of a after thread 2 has read it but before thread 2 has written the new value back to a , the increment operation of thread2 will be overwritten. Using #omp critical is a way to ensure that all the read/increment/write operations are not interrupted by another thread. If you need to parallelize iterations, you can use omp parallel for , for instance to increment all the elements in an array. Typical use: #pragma omp parallel for
    for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
        a[i]++;

A: Setting up the threads and distributing work to them will introduce some overhead. Since you have a small work size (200x300), and the work is simple (only some data copy), the threading overhead can be even larger than your actual work, that's one of the reasons that you don't get performance improvement. Another reason is that your code has poor cache localization due to the use of array of structures (AoS). Especially when you are writing to tmp . For better cache hit performance you could consider use structure of arrays (SoA). In fact you only need to exchange the dimensions from cells[nx*ny][9] to cells[9][nx*ny] Then you will find part of the copy can be done with memcpy() directly. On the other hand, I don't think your code has serious false sharing problem.
A: False sharing occurs when shared cache lines are being modified, i.e. being written to. Given that, you would greatly improve the locality of your code's memory access pattern by simply inverting the operation: instead of performing a scatter operation at each element of cells , do a gather operation at each element of tmp : tmp[ii*nx + jj].s[0] = cells[ii*nx + jj].s[0];
tmp[ii*nx + jj].s[1] = cells[...].s[1];
...
tmp[ii*nx + jj].s[8] = cells[...].s[8]; That way you'll linearise the memory write pattern of each thread, making it much more cache friendly and reducing false sharing at the same time. Also note that your code's performance is mostly limited by the memory bandwidth and therefore for large arrays you might not get any speed-up with more than one thread unless doing so provides more memory bandwidth, e.g. you have a multisocket system with each socket having its own memory controller and each thread executes on cores in different sockets. Your tests with nx of 200 and ny of 300 already requires at least 8,2 MiB of memory and hardly fits in the last-level cache of most desktop processors but still fits in the cache of most server class CPUs. The bigger 2000 x 3000 case is memory-bound for sure.

