A: As already answered by Massimiliano, the easiest way to achieve what you want is to use the sections construct: #pragma omp parallel sections
{
    #pragma omp section
    Func1();
    #pragma omp section
    Func2();
    #pragma omp section
    Func3();
} (when the sections construct is the only thing nested inside a parallel region, both pragmas can be combined as shown) Each section scopes the statement or block immediately following it. E.g. if you'd like to include some more code in a section, you should put it inside a block: #pragma omp section
{
    Func1();
    SomeOtherFunc1();
} A more general way to control what each thread does is to examine the thread ID as returned by omp_get_thread_num() and to branch in accordance. This resembles the way MPI programs are written: #pragma omp parallel
{
    switch (omp_get_thread_num())
    {
        case 0:
            Func1();
            break;
        case 1:
            Func2();
            break;
        case 2:
            Func3();
            break;
        default:
            // Do nothing
     }
} One obvious disadvantage is that it would only work unless there are at least 3 threads in the team that executes the parallel region, i.e. code that relies on thread IDs becomes somewhat non-portable. In contrast, the sections directive works with any number of threads, e.g. if there is only one thread, all sections would be executed sequentially.

A: Your hypothesis is mostly correct, and your calculations are typically "memory bound", that is, there are too few calculations compared to the amount the data that is transfered from/to the memory and the bottleneck is the transfer rate between the CPU and the memory. Thus, using more core doesn't help. On a more recent CPU (particularly on the ones that are designed for servers) you may possibly observe a higher speed-up, because in the recent years the memory bandwidth has increasing faster than the monocore performances. Edit : note that in case obvious memory bound calculations, the best performances are not always obtained with the maximum possible number of threads, as they are all competing for accessing the memory. Using 2 or 4 threads may possibly be (a bit) faster than using 8 threads. Edit 2 : for a more complete answer I am gathering the other possible effects that have been mentioned in the comments (@Homer512) and in the answer by @JerryCoffin: NUMA effects because you have 2 CPUs (slower memory access when one CPU tries accessing the memory that is attached to the other CPU) Thread creation overheads (visible if your arrays are not large enough) (not already mentioned) "cold start" effects (when the CPU is idle the motherborad may lower the frequency, and it can take a bit of time to get back to the base frequency, so that the first calculations are slower) All the above effects partly depend on the way you have written your test code. That's why it's important to show the whole code and not only a small piece. Also: Turbo frequency effect: when a single core is used, the CPU most of time operates at the Turbo frequency, while it's more likely operating at the base frequency when all the cores are used. On your CPUs, the Turbo frequencies would give a ~10% performance bonus to the single core execution.
