A: When inserting to your vectors, you are incrementing iterators past the end of the vectors. For example r.insert(r.begin() + i, 10000000); takes the begining of vector r and increments it i places. However, at that point, vector r is not large enough as the code got parallelized on several threads.
Thus, for example one of the threads might try to insert to location r.begin() + 2 , while r is only 1 element.
You could fix that by pre-allocating your vectors like that: std::vector<double> r(SOME_NUMBER) and then indexing it as r[i] .
Or conversely, you could use push_back instead of insert, but bear in mind that the order of insertion might be shuffled.

