A: If your objective is to parallelize your code using OpenMP and you prefer not to alter the algorithm, then you must utilize either a user-defined reduction or a manual reduction. Below, I've provided an example in response to your request in the comments using manual reduction. // these are the critical variables you have to take care
 float best = LARGE;
 int good[nn];

  #pragma omp parallel
  {  
      // create private variables for each thread
      float private_best = LARGE;
      int private_good[nn];

      #pragma omp for
      for (int first = 0; first < nn; first++) {
        // use the above defined private variables inside the for loop
        // best ---> private_best
        // good ---> private_good
         .....
         .....
      }
      // Find the minimum of 'private_best' and copy it to 'best' and 
      // also copy the 'private_good' array back to 'good' array 
      #pragma omp critical    
      if(private_best < best){
          best = private_best;
          for (int i = 0; i < nn; i++)
              good[i] = private_good[i];          
      }
  }

A: Numerical integration methods such as Runge-Kutta are sequential methods, where each iteration output is the input to the next iteration. You can't calculate the states in parallel since each state needs the previous state solution as an input. E.g., take these first lines from the loop: x1 = private_x[i];
    y1 = private_y[i];
    z1 = private_z[i]; These are used in your RK4 code to eventually calculate the next states: private_x[i + 1] = private_x[i] + dt / 6.0 * (xK1 + 2.0 * xK2 + 2.0 * xK3 + xK4);
    private_y[i + 1] = private_y[i] + dt / 6.0 * (yK1 + 2.0 * yK2 + 2.0 * yK3 + yK4);
    private_z[i + 1] = private_z[i] + dt / 6.0 * (zK1 + 2.0 * zK2 + 2.0 * zK3 + zK4); But you have no reason to believe that in this parallel computation environment the private_x[i], private_y[i], and private_z[i] are actually computed yet. You are using garbage as input to the calculations, and you get garbage out. You must do the iterations in sequence starting from the first one for the RK numerical schemes (Euler, Modified Euler, RK4, etc.) to work.

A: Found a workaround that worked for my case, and thought I'd share it in case someone had a similar problem (unlikely, I know...): Specifying the type of the argument in the lambda function made the compiler understand the code again: #include <iostream>

template <class T>
T add(std::size_t const &maxs) {
  auto step = [&](std::size_t const &maxs) {
    T a = T(0);
#pragma omp declare reduction(MyRed:T                                     \
                              : omp_out += omp_in)                             \
    initializer(omp_priv(omp_orig))
#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic, 10) reduction(MyRed : a)
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s <= maxs; ++s) {
      a += T(1);
    }
    return a;
  };

  return step(maxs);
}

int main() {
  auto a = add<double>(100);

  std::cout << "a=" << a << std::endl;
}

