A: In short, no, it should not (and does not on my machine). First of, OpenMP has a value that sets the number of threads it will use, by default it's set to the number of threads available on your system. So even if this was going to work, the first call would already use all the available threads and the second call would be executed sequentially as a result. This value can be modified by calling omp_set_num_threads(...) , but it is global, so the above behaviour will remain, i.e. the first call will use all the threads it thinks it has available. The easiest way to remedy this is by adding num_threads(...) to the omp pragma and forcing it to ignore that value. Of course you can also use variables in there, so num_threads(x) is completely legal where x is some integer that does not even need to be known at compile time. #pragma omp parallel for num_threads(2) //Let's say we want 2 threads in this for loop However, this alone is not enough because nesting is, by default, disabled. To enable nesting of OMP parallel regions we simply need to call omp_set_nested(true); . It might also be wise to add omp_set_max_active_levels(2); , but this is not needed, the default value seems to be larger (I don't actually know what it is, is might simply be unlimited). So, a simple test program could be something like: #include <omp.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main(void) {
    omp_set_nested(true); //Enables nesting. 
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(2) //We will be using 2 threads for the first for loop.
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        int tid1 = omp_get_thread_num();
#pragma omp parallel for num_threads(2) //And 2 threads for each of the threads in the second loop.
        //This will require a total of 4 threads. 
        for (int j = 0; j < 10; j++) {
            int tid2 = omp_get_thread_num();
            std::string msg =  std::to_string(i) + " " + std::to_string(j) + " " + std::to_string(tid1) + " " + std::to_string(tid2) + "\n";
            std::cerr << msg;
        }
    }
    return 0;
} This will tell use what combination of threads (tid1, tid2) runs each combination of (i, j) , the output is of the form i j tid1 tid2 . If we comment out omp_set_nested(true); we'll notice that tid2 is always 0 , meaning that the inner loop does not execute in parallel. A note regarding the output, both cout and cerr are thread-safe, but that only applies to single calls to the operator << . So using something like std::cout << i << " " << j << ... gives you lots of calls to that operator from different threads and might give you some interleaved parts from different threads (you can try yourself, it won't happen too often but it can). That's why we construct a string and then issue a single call to << . And, as always when trying to run something in parallel, how many threads you want to use in each level will depend on both the type of operations they will be doing and the machine you're running it on. It's probably best to try it and play around a bit if you want to achieve optimal performance. Just make sure the total number of threads (the product of threads in the nested loops) doesn't exceed the number of threads available on your system, that usually slows things down.

A: Just don't parallelize the code inside the for loop - move the parallel region to the outside. This reduces the thread creation and worksharing overhead. Then you can easily apply OpenMP sections : int a_old=1;
int b_old=1;
int c_old=1;
int d_old=1;
#pragma omp parallel sections
{
   #pragma omp section
   for (int i=0; i<1000; i++) {
       a_new = fun(a_old);  //fun() depends only on the value of the argument
       a_old = a_new;
   }
   #pragma omp section
   for (int i=0; i<1000; i++) {
      b_new = fun(b_old);
      b_old = b_new;
   }
   #pragma omp section
   for (int i=0; i<1000; i++) {
      c_new = fun(c_old);
      c_old = c_new;
   }
   #pragma omp section
   for (int i=0; i<1000; i++) {
       d_new = fun(d_old);
       d_old = d_new;
   }
} There is also another simplification: int value[4];
#pragma omp parallel for
for (int abcd = 0; abcd < 4; abcd++) {
    for (int i=0; i<1000; i++) {
        value[abcd] = fun(value[abcd]);
    }
} In either case, you might want to consider adding padding between the values to avoid false sharing if fun executes rather quickly.
