A: What you could do is have a producer-consumer set of tasks such that step 1 produces a buffer, and step 2 consumes that buffer while incorporating the results to your global array. Something like (inside the usual parallel + single ): const int buffer_size = 100000;
type global[buffer_size];
type buffer[2][buffer_size];

for(int i=0;i<10000;i++)
{
    type *buf[buffer_size] = buffer[i % 2];
    #pragma omp task depend(out: [buffer_size]buf)
    {
        <1. write to buf>
    }

    #pragma omp task depend(in: [buffer_size]buf) depend(inout: [buffer_size]global)
    {
        <2. update global using buf>
    }
} This allows to overlap step 2 with step 1, with one iteration of "lag", that is step 1 of iteration i=1 will run concurrently to iteration i=0 of step 2.
You will need one buffer per overlapping iteration, so that each simultaneous operation can happen independently of one another. Now the semantics of buf ( in / out ) are simple, as they are simple procuder-consumer relations. For the global, you need inout , as you update the buffer in-place: get the current array ( in ) and assign new values to it ( out ). This means all the tasks of step 2 will be serialized due to this dependency, meaning that at most 2 iterations can be overlapped (and that there is no point in using more than 2 buffers). This is a limitation of current OpenMP (4.5) tasks, though a proposal for task reductions exists that would resolve this. An alternative way would be to declare global as shared , in which case you need to modify it using atomic operations. You may use a target clause to execute task 1 on the GPU. Make sure that the data from the offloaded device (i.e. the GPU) is copied to the CPU. Inside the task of step 2 you can then parallelize the step 2 itself, using either a parallel for construct, or tasks again.

A: The first thing to do is indeed to isolate which loop parallelizations have the most bad impact, but the last loop there looks very much like you would be experiencing cache thrashing. Simplifying the structure a bit: double vort[Nx][Ny];
// ...
for (int j=1; j<Ny-1; ++j) {
    #pragma omp parallel for
    for (int i=1; i<Nx-1; ++i) {
        vort[i][j] -= f(i, j);
    }
} Any given thread is going to read and update in turn the values in vort at offsets j+k*Ny, j+(k+1)*Ny, j+(k+2)*Ny etc. depending on how the for loop is chunked across the threads. Each of these accesses is going to pull in a cache-line's worth of data to update 8 bytes. And when the outer loop starts again, chances are none of the data you just accessed is still going to be in cache. All things being equal, if you can arrange your array accesses so that you're moving in the direction of the smallest stride (for C arrays, that's the last index), your cache behaviour will be much better. For dimension size 100, the arrays are likely not so big that this makes a huge difference. For e.g. Nx, Ny = 1000, it will likely be devastating to access the array the 'wrong way'. This would give poorer performance in serial code, but I think adding threads to it just makes it that much worse. That all said, the amount of computation done in each of these inner loops is quite small; there's a good chance you're going to be constrained by memory bandwidth regardless. Addendum Just to be explicit, the 'right' loop access would look like: for (int i=1; i<Nx-1; ++i) {
    for (int j=1; j<Ny-1; ++j) {
        vort[i][j] -= f(i, j);
    }
} And to parallelize it, you can allow the compiler to better chunk the data across threads by using the collapse directive: #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2)
for (int i=1; i<Nx-1; ++i) {
    for (int j=1; j<Ny-1; ++j) {
        vort[i][j] -= f(i, j);
    }
} Lastly, in order to avoid false sharing (threads treading on each other's cache lines), it's good to make sure that two adjacent rows of the array don't share data in the same cache line. One could make sure that each row is aligned on to a multiple of the cache-line size in memory, or more simply just add padding to the end of each row. double vort[Nx][Ny+8]; // 8 doubles ~ 64 bytes (Assuming a cache-line of 64 bytes, this should suffice.)

