A: If we declare a static variable inside of a function (it doesn't matter inline or not), the compiler will generate a global variable with a unique name (usually combined of the function name and the variable name). The difference between static function variables and global variables is in scope of visibility only.
So version 2 is not thread safe. Let's compare version 1 and 3. Version 1 is a bit faster due to X being a register, and it's not necessary to store it to main memory. Speed difference doesn't depend on inline or openmp , so I'll show a simpler example: #include <math.h>

double func1(int i){
    double X;
    X=M_SQRT2*i;
    return X;
}

double func2(int i){
    static double X;
    X=M_SQRT2*i;
    return X;
} Compile it: gcc -S -O3 test.c Here -S is to generate assembler file test.s and -O3 is to enable optimization. func1:
.LFB3:
    .cfi_startproc
    cvtsi2sd %edi, %xmm0
    mulsd .LC0(%rip), %xmm0
    ret
    .cfi_endproc

func2:
.LFB4:
    .cfi_startproc
    cvtsi2sd    %edi, %xmm0
    mulsd   .LC0(%rip), %xmm0
    movsd   %xmm0, X.2782(%rip)
    ret
    .cfi_endproc We may see that func2 is one line longer.
Instruction movsd   %xmm0, X.2782(%rip) stores result to main memory. X.2782 is our generated global variable. So version 1 is better.

