A: When temp3 is an array ( mpz_t temp3[ll] ) and you declare it private then OpenMP makes private versions of the the array for each thread (i.e. it allocates separate private memory from the stack of each thread).  However, in the second case using malloc temp3 is only a pointer (arrays and pointers are not the same thing) and when you pass a private pointer then OpenMP only makes private versions of the pointer for each thread (it allocates no memory). In the first case the crash could be due to the array being larger than the stack size.  In the second case the private versions of the pointers are never initialized (as they would be with firstprivate) so they point to unknown memory addresses which likely causes the crash. The solution is simple. Don't declare temp3 private.  It should be shared. You could try leaving temp3 as private when it's a pointer if you use firstprivate but I don't think that's a good idea. #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int main() {
    int x[10];
    printf("%p\n", x);
    #pragma omp parallel private(x) 
    {
        printf("%p\n", x);
    }

    int* y = (int*)malloc(10*sizeof(int));
    #pragma omp parallel firstprivate(y)
    {
        printf("%p\n", y);
    }
}

A: With ray tracing you should use schedule(dynamic) .  Besides that I would suggest fusing the loop #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) {
for(int n=0; n<((viewport.xvmax - viewport.xvmin)*(viewport.yvmax - viewport.yvmin); n++) {
    int i = n/(viewport.yvmax - viewport.yvmin);
    int j = n%(viewport.yvmax - viewport.yvmin)
    //...
} Also, why are you setting the number of threads?  Just use the default which should be set to the number of logical cores.  If you have Hyper Threading ray tracing is one of the algorithms that will benefit from Hyper Threading so you don't want to set the number of threads to the number of physical cores. In addition to using MIMD with OpenMP I would suggest looking into using SIMD for ray tracing.  See Ingo Wald's PhD thesis for an example on how to do this http://www.sci.utah.edu/~wald/PhD/ .  Basically you shoot four (eight) rays in one SSE (AVX) register and then go down the ray tree for each ray in parallel.  However, if one ray finishes you hold it and wait until all four are finished (this is similar to what is done on the GPU).  There have been many papers written since which have more advanced tricks based on this idea.

