A: MPI is a method to communicate between nodes of a computing cluster. It enables one motherboard to talk to another. MPI is for clusters and large computing tasks, it is not for parallelizing desktop applications. Communications in MPI are done by explicitly sending and receiving data. Unlike OpenMP, there is no #pragma that will automatically facilitate parallelization. Also there is something really messed up about the code that you posted, specifically, it is a C program that acts like a bash script. #!/bin/bash
N=10
for i in `seq 1 $N`;
do
./program $i &
done On many clusters calls to system will execute only on the host node, resulting in no speedup and io problems. The command you showed is wholly unworkable.
