A: With parallel programming, there are a number of factors that must be taken into consideration. First, you have the non-negligible startup cost (overhead) of creating separate threads/setting up multiple processes. For this reason, adding parallelism typically makes things run less efficiently unless you have sufficient data that running multiple threads will actually improve the overall runtime. Secondly, these tasks must be scheduled onto the number of cores you have available. If you have 4 cores and 64 tasks, those 64 tasks need to be scheduled onto the cores - a nontrivial task if each task takes a different amount of time to complete. Thirdly, if there is any interference between threads, then that can slow things down, especially with a large number of threads. Additionally, there is the aspect of skew, where the slowest task is the bottleneck - until the slowest task finishes, the entire set of processes is not considered finished. These are just some of the factors that should be taken into consideration.

