A: You simply forget to distribute the work across the threads. You create a parallel region with the parallel directive, which means a number of threads (default: the numbers of cores your OS sees) are created from this point on. Each thread execute all iterations (i.e num_vertices * num_vertices iterations) in your code below, which explains why you don't get any speedup. #pragma omp parallel reduction(+: Q)
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < num_vertices; i++)
    {
        for (uint32_t j = 0; j < num_vertices; j++)
        {
        }
     } This happens because you don't tell OpenMP to share the work (i.e split the iteration set across the available threads). Work sharing of a for loop is requested with #pragma omp for just before the loop i for instance, which would distribute equally the iterations i over the threads. Without a schedule clause, thread 0 deals with iterations [0,..,num_vertices/T-1], thread 1 has iterations [num_vertices/T, .., 2*num_vertices/T-1], ..., where T is the number of threads in the parallel region. As your iteration domain is a square, you could increase the number of iterations to distribute (in case num_vertices is small) with #pragma omp for collapse(2) to distribute iterations in i and j . Finally, as your #pragma omp for would immediately follow your #pragma omp parallel , people usually use the shorthand parallel for to combine the two directives. Your program would then read: #pragma omp parallel for reduction(+: Q)
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < num_vertices; i++)
    {
        for (uint32_t j = 0; j < num_vertices; j++)
        {
        }
     }

