A: No. It is not possible to cancel an ordered loop. A loop construct that is canceled must not have an ordered clause. (cf. 2.14.1 of the OpenMP standard) One of the workaround to emulate cancellation is to add a skip at the beginning of the loop, e.g. #pragma omp parallel for ordered schedule(dynamic) shared(prime_counter)
for (candidate = start; candidate <= end; candidate += 2) {
  if (prime_counter >= max_primes) {
    continue;
  }
  if (is_prime(candidate)) { However, that is not yet a thread safe access to prime_counter . In order to avoid race conditions, you must do something along the lines of: int local_prime_counter;
  #pragma omp atomic read
  local_prime_counter = prime_counter;
  if (local_prime_counter >= max_primes)

  ...

