A: You don't need to do anything special, almost . Just write code for a compute-intensive task and put it inside a parallel region. Then indicate what number of threads you want. In order to do that, you use omp_set_dynamic(0) to disable dynamic threads (this helps to achieve the number of threads you want, but it still won't be guaranteed), then omp_set_num_threads(NUM_THREADS) to indicate what number of threads you want. Then each thread will clone the task you indicate in the code. Simple as that. const int NUM_THREADS = 100;
omp_set_dynamic(0);
omp_set_num_threads(NUM_THREADS);
#pragma omp parallel
{
    // How many threads did we really get? Let's write it once only.
    #pragma omp single
    {
         cout << "using " << omp_get_num_threads() << " threads." << std::endl;
    }
    // write some compute-intensive code here
    // (be sure to print the result at the end, so that
    // the compiler doesn't throw away useless instructions)
}
