A: False sharing only occurs on the boundary between two neighbouring threads. So that is not a problem for omp parallel for . Note that if c is not aligned to cache-boundaries, you actually introduce false sharing on every cacheline. That said, I think the problem is prefetching. Limiting factor Try running the parallel for version with OMP_NUM_THREADS=p OMP_PLACES=cores and several values of p . On my system processors, bandwidth (GB/s)
1, 18
2, 21
3, 20
4, 21
5, 20
6, 20 the reason the speedup is bad is because this benchmark is limited by the bandwidth between RAM and CPU, which is shared by all cores. Prefetch In order to hide the latency of moving a cacheline from RAM to the CPU, they will already start sending the next cacheline over (called a prefetch). However, if you end up not needing this cacheline, you will have wasted bandwidth. CPUs will typically guess the next cacheline is the one adjacent to it. They will try to recognize patterns such as in your code as well, but in my experience most CPUs are not good at this. Prefetching adjacent cachelines is suboptimal. You can see the performance getting gradually closer when increasing the chunk size.

