A: The problem is that there is a race condition in this line: C[i * n + j] += ... Different threads can read and write the same memory location ( C[i * n + j] ) simultaneously, which causes data race. In omp2 this data race can occur, but not in omp3 . The solution is (as suggested by @Victor Eijkhout) is to reorder your loops, use a local variable to calculate the sum of the innermost loop. In this case C[i * n + j] is updated only once, so you got rid of data race and the outermost loop can be parallelized (which gives the best performance): #pragma omp parallel for
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
            double sum=0;            
            for (int ki = 0; ki < k; ++ki) {            
                sum += A[i * k + ki] * B[ki * n + j];
            }
            C[i * n + j] +=sum;
        }        
    } Note that you can use collapse(2) clause, which may increase the performance.

