A: I don't think it's possible to increment iteration elsewhere than inside the loop body. For instance, this is not allowed: std::atomic<int> iteration{0};
#pragma omp parallel for 
for (int64_t ip = 0; ip < num_voxels; ip++, iteration++) { ... since OpenMP requires so-called Canonical Loop Form where the increment expression may not update multiple variables (see Section 2.6 of OpenMP 4.5 Spcification). Also I would strongly advise against incrementing iteration within each loop, since it would be very inefficient (atomic memory operations = memory fences and cache contention). I would prefer, e.g.: int64_t iteration = 0;
int64_t local_iteration = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for firstprivate(local_iteration) 
for (int64_t ip = 0; ip < num_voxels; ip++) {
{
   ... // calc stuff      
   if (++local_iteration % 1024 == 0) { // modulo using bitwise AND
     #pragma omp atomic
     iteration += 1024;
   }
   // output stuff
   // if thread == 0:
   // Progress(iteration / num_voxels * 100);
} And, output only if progress in percents changes. This might be also tricky, since you need to read iteration atomically and you likely don't want to do that in each iteration. A possible solution, which also saves a lot of cycles regarding "expensive" integer division: int64_t iteration = 0;
int64_t local_iteration = 0;
int64_t last_progress = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for firstprivate(local_iteration) 
for (int64_t ip = 0; ip < num_voxels; ip++) {
{
   ... // calc stuff      
   if (++local_iteration % 1024 == 0) { // modulo using bitwise AND
      #pragma omp atomic
      iteration += 1024;

      // output stuff:
      if (omp_get_thread_num() == 0) {
         int64_t progress;
         #pragma omp atomic read
         progress = iteration;
         progress = progress / num_voxels * 100;
         if (progress != last_prgoress) {
            Progress(progress);
            last_progress = progress;
         }
      }
   }
}

A: This way is "cool" and works in parallel. Note that taskwait does allow the thread to execute some other task, e.g. a task created by it's own child task. Just to illustrate a possible execution schedule: * creating a task , # actually waiting for all child tasks
           ------- time --->
Thread 1   {fib(4): **b      {fib(2): ** {fib(1): ret 1}   # ret 1+0}         # ret 2+1
Thread 2            {fib(3): ** {fib(1): ret 1}                     # ret 1+1}
Thread 3             {fib(2): ** {fib(0): ret 0} ret 1+0}
Thread 4                      {fib(1): ret 1}{fib(0): ret 0} Of course this the code is still horribly inefficient compared to iterative computation. Also in actual code using tasking on recursive algorithms, the creation of new tasks should be stopped after a certain recursion depth to manage the task creation overhead.
A: Below is an alternative parallel solution which directly calculates fib(n) and fib(n+1) using the golden ratio and then calculates the rest of the sequence sequentially for each thread. I got the direct solution from rosettacode . The problem is that the direct calculations overflows for n>72 so this method is almost certainly slower than calculating the first 71 numbers sequentially and in any case 64-bit integers overflows for n>92 . #include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <inttypes.h>
#include <omp.h>

uint64_t fib(unsigned m) { // Direct Calculation, correct for abs(m) <= 71
  double sqrt5r =  1.0 / sqrt(5.0);      
  double golden = (1.0 + sqrt(5.0))/2.0;
  return rint(pow(golden, m)*sqrt5r);
}

void fib_sequence(unsigned n, uint64_t *f) {
  #pragma omp parallel
  {
    size_t t  = omp_get_thread_num();
    size_t nt = omp_get_num_threads();
    int x0 = (t+0)*n/nt;
    int x1 = (t+1)*n/nt;
    f[x0 + 0] = fib(x0 + 0);
    f[x0 + 1] = fib(x0 + 1);
    for(int i=x0+2; i<x1; i++) {
      f[i] = f[i-2] + f[i-1];
    }
  }
}

int main(void) {
  int n = 71;
  uint64_t f1[n], f2[n];
  for(int i=0; i<n; i++) f1[i] = fib(i);
  fib_sequence(n, f2);
  for(int i=0; i<n; i++) if(f1[i] != f2[i]) printf("%d\n", i);
  for(int i=0; i<n; i++) printf("F%d: %" PRId64 " %" PRId64 "\n", i, f1[i], f2[i]);      
}
A: One of my friends did a cool trick when he did parallelization of finding n Fibonacci numbers. We all know that kth fibonacci number can be found in log(k) time using matrix exponentation . Also fibonacci(j) only depends on fibonacci(j - 1) and fibonacci(j - 2) Suppose you have four threads, then first thread will be given task of finding 0 to (n/4)-1 fibonacci numbers iteratively.
Now second thread has to find fibonacci numbers from n/4 to 2n/4 - 1 but for that it needs fibo(n/4 - 1). Well now the trick is this thread will calculate fibo(n/4) and fibo(n/4 + 1) using matrix exponentation in O(log(n)) time (Not much time right and clever too because we are not waiting for first thread's end result). Now as soon as the second thread gets fibo(n/4) and fibo(n/4 + 1), it will in parallel with other threads find fibonacci numbers from n/4 to 2n/4 - 1. Similarly the third thread will first using matrix exponentation find fibo(2n/4) and fibo(2n/4 + 1) and it will in parallel with other threads find fibonacci numbers from 2n/4 to 3n/4 - 1. And the last fourth thread will do similarly. Now if you ask for performance, each thread among available p theads will spend at most 2log(n) time and do n/p iterations that will take O(n/p) time. So each thread will do at most the work of O(n/p + log(n)) and the speedup would be pn / n + plog(n) Hope that helps!

A: Forget about using a reduction in this case, the overhead it would incur (100000+ elements in the reduction variable) compared to the effect of the parallelization would likely kill most of the gain. Just stick to simple parallelization constructs. Ideally, what you'd want would be to parallelize the j loop as it carries no dependencies between iterations. So you can just do it like this: #pragma omp parallel
for ( int i = 0; i < 500; i++ ) {
    #pragma omp for
    for ( int j = i; j < 102342; j++ ) {
        Output[j] += staticConstant[i] * data[j-i];
    }
} For a code as simple as this one, this should be just enough. Now, you might want go a step further and try to swap the i and j loops to improve the parallelization effectiveness (not sure it'll make much of a difference though). For that, you'll need to remove the dependency to i in the j loop initialization. Here is a way of doing it: // first let's do the dependent iterations in j
for ( int i = 0; i < 500; i++ ) {
    for ( int j = i; j < 500; j++ ) {
        Output[j] += staticConstant[i] * data[j-i];
    }
}
// then all the other iterations, and swap the i and j loops
// now we can parallelize no problem
#pragma omp parallel for
for ( int j = 500; j < 102342; j++ ) {
    for ( int i = 0; i < 500; i++ ) {
        Output[j] += staticConstant[i] * data[j-i];
    }
}
