A: To make a tree thread-safe for write operations (like add a node in your example) I can think of only the locking algorithms - e.g. Two-phase locking . These structures are used in Databases for instance. The idea would be to go down the tree, figure out where the node needs to be added, which (all) other parent nodes it will affect, wait for a lock on those, lock them, do the add operation and unlock. This will always keep the tree in a consistent state while allowing concurrent add operations in different parts of the tree. Therefore, before you even consider to implement this, take a look at how you add the data into the tree. If most additions would be conflicting then the overhead from locking wouldn't outweigh the gains in speed-up. A few more comments. What @Joseph Franciscus meant with doing the bulk of your computations in parallel and then sequentially add all nodes to the tree should work well if you aren't expecting the amount of nodes in order of billions. You could, however, expand on his idea. You can implement something similar to the parallel Produce-Consume pattern. An arbitrary number of worker threads would work on creating the bodies and put the results in a thread-safe queue and one thread only (!) would be adding them. This way you can make both jobs intertwine with each other and get even more work done in parallel. PS. The barrier after omp parallel for is implicit, you don't need to put it there AFAIK. Edit :
I was thinking maybe a bit of pseudo-C-code could help: #pragma omp parallel sections num_threads(2)
{
  #pragma omp section
  {
    while (true) {
      if (queue_notEmpty()){
        if (node is last) break;
        node = queue_front(); queue_pop();
        tree->addNode(node);
      }
    }
  }
  #pragma omp section
  {
     #pragma omp parallel for
     for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        node = init_node(...);
        queue_push(node);
     }
  }
} This will first result in two threads, each taking one of the sections. Then in the second section further threads will be spawned, you could also control that with a num_thread property. The only caveat here I can think of is how to make the thread putting nodes into the tree end. You could put like a special node into the queue that signalizes that no more nodes will be added. The pseudocode I wrote also does so called active waiting. It asks the queue all the time whether it is or isn't empty. You could get rid of it by signaling the consumer thread with a semaphore. Depends on how much the thread needs to wait for data. you can experiment with that, too. The standard library queues/deques aren't thread-safe so make sure to either implement your own or use a library made for usage in parallel scenarios. Hope it works out!
