A: The OpenMP 4.0 specification was finalised and published several days ago here . It still mandates that parallel loops should be in the canonical form (§2.6, p.51): for ( init-expr ; test-expr ; incr-expr ) structured-block The standard allows for containers that provide random-access iterators to be used in all of the expressions, e.g.: #pragma omp parallel for
for (it = v.begin(); it < v.end(); it++)
{
   ...
} If you still insist on using the C++11 syntactic sugar, and if it takes a (comparatively) lot of time to process each element of stl_container , then you could use the single-producer tasking pattern: #pragma omp parallel
{
   #pragma omp single
   {
      for (auto x : stl_container)
      {
         #pragma omp task
         {
            // Do something with x, e.g.
            compute(x);
         }
      }
   }
} Tasking induces certain overhead so it would make no sense to use this pattern if compute(x); takes very little time to complete.
